{"x":0.56342,"y":0.68938,"w":0.17668,"h":0.03263,"fs":0.01277,"n":8,"k":0,"t":"w nR T T V V net h l"},{"x":0.89431,"y":0.69429,"w":0.05176,"h":0.01527,"fs":0.01277,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"[13.19]"},{"x":0.59794,"y":0.69707,"w":0.14829,"h":0.02624,"fs":0.01277,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"A = − − ( )ln"},{"x":0.36765,"y":0.76301,"w":0.13174,"h":0.02115,"fs":0.01788,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Heat E ngines"},{"x":0.36764,"y":0.79038,"w":0.58298,"h":0.10692,"fs":0.01277,"n":7,"k":1,"t":"The Carnot cycle is an idealized model for a heat engine. When a certain amount of heat, qAB, is added to the system at the higher temperature, Th, a net amount of work, 2wnet, is obtained from the system. In addition, some heat qCD is discharged at the lower temperature, but this energy is “degraded” and is no longer available for use in the engine. The ­efficiency, e, of such an engine is the ratio of the negative of the net work done on the system, 2wnet, to the heat added along the high- temperature isothermal path:"},{"x":0.61862,"y":0.9062,"w":0.0582,"h":0.02335,"fs":0.01277,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"e = −w"},{"x":0.67713,"y":0.91189,"w":0.01401,"h":0.00973,"fs":0.0083,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"net"},{"x":0.66846,"y":0.92978,"w":0.01404,"h":0.00973,"fs":0.0083,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"AB"},{"x":0.11598,"y":0.97765,"w":0.76851,"h":0.01618,"fs":0.00639,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Copyright 2011 Cengage Learning. All Rights Reserved. May not be copied, scanned, or duplicated, in whole or in part. Due to electronic rights, some third party content may be suppressed from the eBook and/or eChapter(s). Editorial review has deemed that any suppressed content does not materially affect the overall learning experience. Cengage Learning reserves the right to remove additional content at any time if subsequent rights restrictions require it."}]}